-      <p class="eyebrow">Our story</p>
-      <h1>Code hosting that can't be taken away.</h1>
-      <p class="about-lede">
-        ForkMesh began with a loss — and a refusal to let it happen again.
-      </p>
-
-      <h2>The day the code disappeared</h2>
-      <p>
-        One ordinary morning, <strong>Md Kaif Ansari</strong> opened GitHub to
-        find his repositories gone. No warning he could act on, no second copy to
-        fall back on. Years of work — side projects, experiments, the small tools
-        he'd quietly built for himself and others — vanished behind someone
-        else's policy and someone else's servers.
-      </p>
-      <p>
-        The code wasn't deleted because it was wrong. It was deleted because it
-        lived in exactly one place, and that place answered to no one but itself.
-      </p>
-
-      <blockquote class="pullquote">
-        “If a single account can erase years of work overnight, then we never
-        really owned our code — we were only borrowing it.”
-      </blockquote>
-
-      <h2>So he built the antidote</h2>
-      <p>
-        ForkMesh is what Kaif wished he'd had that morning: a network where your
-        code doesn't sit on one company's servers, waiting to be locked out. Every
-        repository lives on real machines run by real people — mirrored, signed,
-        and streamed peer-to-peer. No central forge holds your objects hostage.
-        Take one node offline and the mesh carries on.
-      </p>
-      <p>
-        Around that foundation grew everything a modern project needs: issues and
-        milestones, pull requests and releases, encrypted chat, real CI, and AI
-        agents that can pick up an issue and ship a fix — all without surrendering
-        your work to a single point of failure. And because the people who keep
-        the network alive deserve to be paid, ForkMesh has rewards and bounties
-        built right in.
-      </p>
